<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Jefferson asked for a same-day visit for a head cold. Four days ago, after several colleagues had colds, he had a scratchy throat, followed by a runny and then blocked nose, sneezing, and a dull pressure over his cheeks and forehead that is worse when he bends forward. He has a slight cough and felt warm last night. He has no tooth pain, eye swelling, stiff neck, confusion or high fever. The pressure is different from his migraine: it is mild, constant, not throbbing, and without light sensitivity or nausea. He had one migraine attack two days ago, which he treated with sumatriptan and naproxen and which was gone in two hours. He has taken propranolol 80 mg each evening for two and a half weeks. He wants to know whether this is a sinus infection that needs antibiotics, and whether he can take a cold remedy from the pharmacy. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Four days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Nose, cheeks and forehead</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Four days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Blocked nose and dull, constant pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Mild to moderate</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Constant; worse when bending forward</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Colds among his colleagues; propranolol for two and a half weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Better with steam; worse bending forward</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Sneezing, slight cough, felt warm; no tooth pain, eye swelling, stiff neck or confusion</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

