  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Common cold (J00)</content>. A viral cold of four days. Bacterial sinusitis is unlikely: symptoms are under ten days, are not worsening after improving, and there is no high fever or facial swelling. Antibiotics would not help. Facial pressure with a cold is expected and differs from his migraine. [S]<list><item>Saline nasal rinses, steam, fluids and honey for the cough.</item><item>Avoid decongestant tablets (pseudoephedrine or phenylephrine), which can raise blood pressure and pulse and blunt the effect of propranolol.</item><item>Avoid combination cold remedies that contain analgesics or caffeine, which add to his acute-medicine days.</item><item>Return if symptoms last ten days, worsen after improving, or bring a high fever, eye swelling, a severe one-sided headache or a stiff neck.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Migraine without aura on propranolol (G43.009)</content>. One migraine attack in the past week, treated well; acute medicine on one day so far this month. No red flags. [S]<list><item>Continue propranolol 80 mg each evening.</item><item>If a cold triggers an attack, use sumatriptan with naproxen as before and record the day in the diary.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (2021); Clinical Practice Guideline (Update): Adult Sinusitis (2015)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-week review in ten days. Return sooner as above. Next scheduled visit 1790-06-18: six-week review, four weeks after starting propranolol: tolerability, attacks, acute medicine days and the wrist. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
